<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  polym2: make the hourly Parquet export fit in a bounded ClickHouse.

  Mounted into /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/ by docker-compose.infra.polym2.yml.
  These are query-level settings, so they belong in a profile rather than in the
  server config next door.

  The exporter runs one `SELECT * ... FORMAT Parquet` per hour. ClickHouse buffers
  a whole row group before writing it, and the defaults are 1M rows / 512 MB. At
  our volume a full hour is ~153M rows, and on 2026-08-05 that combination hit
  the memory ceiling every single time: the exporter retried the same hour every
  60 seconds for hours and produced nothing.

  Measured on hour 16 of that day, exporting with the ceiling at 3.5 GiB:

    defaults          HTTP 500 immediately
    100k rows / 64 MB truncated at 6.5 MB
    25k rows / 16 MB  truncated at 416 MB
    10k rows / 8 MB   truncated at 762 MB

  Smaller groups get progressively further because less is held at once, but they
  do not fix it alone. The ceiling had to come up as well, since the export
  competes with ongoing inserts and merges rather than running on a quiet server.
  Both halves are needed; neither is sufficient.

  Careful with comments here: XML forbids a double hyphen inside one, and
  ClickHouse refuses to start on a malformed config rather than skipping it.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <profiles>
        <default>
            <output_format_parquet_row_group_size>10000</output_format_parquet_row_group_size>
            <output_format_parquet_row_group_size_bytes>8388608</output_format_parquet_row_group_size_bytes>

            <!-- The one that actually made the export complete.

                 With the default parallelism ClickHouse reads far faster than
                 the HTTP client can write the result to disk, and buffers the
                 difference: failing exports reported 5.5 to 6.0 GiB of query
                 memory. Held to one thread the read paces itself against the
                 writer and the same hour finishes at 76 MiB.

                 Measured on hour 16, 153M rows, with the ceiling at 7.2 GiB:

                   default threads  failed at 3.64 GB after 188 s
                   max_threads=2    failed at 1.33 GB after 48 s
                   max_threads=1    COMPLETE, 5.54 GB in 394 s

                 Slower, and it does not matter: the exporter has an hour to
                 write an hour, and 394 seconds is well inside that.

                 Set on the profile rather than per query so the exporter picks
                 it up without touching upstream's Python. Nothing else queries
                 this server. -->
            <max_threads>1</max_threads>
        </default>
    </profiles>
</clickhouse>
